 along the eastern bank of the Chenab River has facilitated its evolution as a critical hub for trade, culture, and religion throughout history, particularly during the Classical era, when it became a melting pot for various civilizations including the Indus Valley Civilization, the Persian Empire, and later the Islamic Caliphates. The city is often referred to as the 'City of Saints,' reflecting its rich tapestry of spiritual heritage, which includes the revered shrine of Baha-ud-din Zakariya, a prominent Sufi saint, who lived and taught there in the 13th century.

